@*
   FILE ROLE: Demonstrates the custom AlertTagHelper and GravatarTagHelper in action.
              Also covers @removeTagHelper, @tagHelperPrefix, and the lifecycle of
              a custom tag helper (discovery → instantiation → property binding → Process).

   SECTIONS IN THIS FILE:
     1. AlertTagHelper usage    — <alert type="..."> all severity variants
     2. Dismissible alert       — <alert type="..." dismissible>
     3. GravatarTagHelper usage — <img-gravatar email="..." size="...">
     4. @removeTagHelper        — how and when to opt-out of a tag helper
     5. @tagHelperPrefix        — requiring a namespace prefix
*@
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Custom Tag Helpers";
}

<h2>Custom Tag Helpers</h2>

@* ============================================================
   SECTION 1: AlertTagHelper — <alert type="...">...</alert>

   Source: TagHelpers/AlertTagHelper.cs
   Registered via: @addTagHelper *, TagHelpers  (in _ViewImports.cshtml)

   At render time the Razor engine:
     1. Encounters <alert type="success">
     2. Looks up the registered class: AlertTagHelper (via [HtmlTargetElement("alert")])
     3. Instantiates AlertTagHelper (supports constructor DI)
     4. Sets AlertType = "success"  (bound from type="success" via [HtmlAttributeName])
     5. Calls ProcessAsync(context, output)
     6. The method changes <alert> → <div class="alert alert-success" role="alert">
     7. Child content is read via GetChildContentAsync() and emitted inside <div>

   Rendered HTML:
     <div class="alert alert-success" role="alert">
       Your message was sent successfully!
     </div>
   ============================================================ *@
<h4>1. Alert Variants (AlertTagHelper)</h4>

<alert type="success">
    Your message was sent successfully!
</alert>

<alert type="info">
    Fill in the form below to get in touch.
</alert>

<alert type="warning">
    Please review your information before submitting.
</alert>

<alert type="danger">
    An error occurred. Please try again later.
</alert>

@* ============================================================
   SECTION 2: DISMISSIBLE ALERT — <alert type="..." dismissible>

   The "dismissible" attribute is a bool property on AlertTagHelper.
   Mere PRESENCE of the attribute (no value) sets Dismissible = true.

   The tag helper adds Bootstrap dismiss classes:
     class="alert alert-warning alert-dismissible fade show"
   AND appends a <button class="btn-close" ...> via PostContent.

   The rendered HTML:
     <div class="alert alert-warning alert-dismissible fade show" role="alert">
       This is a dismissible warning.
       <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="alert" ...></button>
     </div>

   Bootstrap JavaScript (data-bs-dismiss="alert") handles the hide animation.
   ============================================================ *@
<h4>2. Dismissible Alert</h4>

<alert type="warning" dismissible>
    This warning can be dismissed by the user.
</alert>

<alert type="danger" dismissible>
    Critical error — click &times; to dismiss.
</alert>

@* ============================================================
   SECTION 3: GravatarTagHelper — <img-gravatar email="..." size="..." alt="...">

   Source: TagHelpers/GravatarTagHelper.cs
   The [HtmlTargetElement("img-gravatar")] attribute is required because the hyphenated
   element name cannot be reliably inferred from the class name alone.

   Process() (synchronous — no child content):
     1. Lowercases and trims the email
     2. Computes MD5 hash of the result (MD5.HashData — static, no IDisposable)
     3. Builds the Gravatar URL: https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/{hash}?s={size}&d=identicon
     4. Replaces <img-gravatar> → <img> with src, alt, width, height attributes

   Rendered HTML:
     <img src="https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b642b4217b34b1e8d3bd915fc65c4452?s=80&d=identicon"
          alt="Profile picture" width="80" height="80" />

   Note: &d=identicon means "use a geometric fallback if no Gravatar is registered."
   Other fallback options: mp (mystery person), retro, robohash, blank.
   ============================================================ *@
<h4>3. Gravatar Tag Helper</h4>

<p>Default size (80px), default alt text:</p>
<img-gravatar email="user@example.com" />

<p>Custom size and alt:</p>
<img-gravatar email="admin@example.com" size="120" alt="Admin avatar" />

<p>Unknown email → identicon fallback:</p>
<img-gravatar email="no-gravatar-registered@test.invalid" size="64" alt="Identicon" />

@* ============================================================
   SECTION 4: @removeTagHelper — OPTING OUT OF A SPECIFIC TAG HELPER

   Syntax (in a .cshtml file or _ViewImports.cshtml):
     @removeTagHelper FullyQualifiedClassName, AssemblyName

   After @removeTagHelper, the matching element reverts to plain HTML.
   Useful when:
     - A built-in tag helper conflicts with a third-party one
     - You need <form action="..."> to render as-is (without MVC route generation)
     - A partial view should be isolated from the parent's tag helper registrations

   Example — disable FormTagHelper in this view only:
     @removeTagHelper Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers.FormTagHelper,
                      Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.TagHelpers
   After this, <form asp-action="..."> in THIS view is NOT processed — it emits
   <form asp-action="Submit"> as literal HTML (asp-action is not an HTML attribute).

   You can also @removeTagHelper in a nested _ViewImports.cshtml to scope the
   removal to a specific folder rather than a single view.

   Order matters:
     @addTagHelper and @removeTagHelper are processed in declaration order.
     A @removeTagHelper after an @addTagHelper wins; re-adding after a removal also works.
   ============================================================ *@

@* ============================================================
   SECTION 5: @tagHelperPrefix — REQUIRING A NAMESPACE PREFIX

   Syntax (in _ViewImports.cshtml):
     @tagHelperPrefix  th:

   After this, all tag helpers require the "th:" prefix:
     <th:form asp-action="Submit">...</th:form>  → works
     <form asp-action="Submit">...</form>         → NOT processed (passes through)

   Prefix characters allowed: any valid XML name start character.
   Common prefixes: "th:", "mvc:", "asp:" (but asp: clashes with built-in attributes).

   Use case: migrating a legacy frontend with custom <form> or <a> handling that
   must NOT be intercepted by MVC tag helpers.  Prefix isolates MVC markup explicitly.
   ============================================================ *@
